A pilgrimage is a gathering, and [[Org/The Harlequins/index|the Harlequins]] are known to organize them. Invitations are blue paper reading that through connections, guile, or something perhaps more unsavory, the bearer has acquired an invitation, with the rest in gold text; each invites one and names no recipient. [[Org/Iconoclasm/People/Anaïs Marchal|Anaïs Marchal]] has been to a pilgrimage before, in [[Geography/Istria/index|Istria]]. The Party believes the gathering is connected to the six gods said to be coming to destroy [[Færrin]].

The current pilgrimage is held at [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]], a dead mining town a little under a day's drive out of [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]]. Its centerpiece is the Istrian strider the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]], a platform on eight legs with buildings on top, now complete and taking its pilgrims aboard. The host and his Istrian companions built it, and the host means to take it on a pilgrimage to the Void Side Wall. Crossing the town line flattens every raiment to the same plain gray, so that no one can be read for the god behind their blessing.

The host is a tall lanky figure plainly not human. He wears a black pinstriped suit and a plain white mask with crescent eyes and a wide black toothless grin, and he speaks over a crowd without raising his voice. The strider cannot hold the some four hundred guests of the opening night, so a place aboard goes to whoever holds a [[Phenomena/Keystone|keystone]] when it leaves, and the host cares nothing for how a stone was come by. He keeps to himself, receiving guests one at a time by request, and meets them all together at a banquet the evening the strider departs. He has warned of festivities on the road.

On the opening night the main street was dressed for a party: electric fairy lights on the old lamp posts, banners reading "Pilgrimage Welcome," tables of hors d'oeuvres and champagne, and hundreds of guests, some with three or four armed attendants. Guests handed in their invitations at a table and were given plain white masks, all alike, that pressed to any face and changed nothing of the wearer. Weapons were not taken from anyone. The Harlequins served drinks and hors d'oeuvres, and their platters carried a faint whiff of sulfur.

The games along the street pay in hints toward the keystones. The fortune-telling booth reads Harrow fortunes and then gives a hint. A barker before a rainbow-prism box sets seven walls of seven colors around a key, with seven potions to pass them and a riddle to work out the order. A table of dice takes a fifty-gold buy-in against a hint. A Harlequin with a painter's palette stands before blank wooden mannequins.

Two signposted paths lead out of town. The crossed-swords path leads to the disused train yard, where a Harlequin at an observation deck spins a wheel of colors and rings a bell, and creatures come out of the smoke to fight whoever goes down. Those who survive are led to a foreman's office and given three questions. The Harlequin there will not give a keystone's location, nor cross streets to it. The fairy-ring path leads to a parking lot behind a shuttered store, where a Harlequin runs an association game: three mushroom rings set in a Venn diagram, one player to each, and items to be laid in the four overlaps that connect the players. What connects them earns a soft ding; what does not brings an anvil, a pit of acid or a volley of darts.

Among the other guests are an elf and a gnome. The elf is [[Org/Protectorate/People/Tamara McKay|Tamara McKay]] of the [[Org/Protectorate/index|Protectorate]], and the two of them are after keystone 8.

The [[Org/Embercall Industries/index|Embercall]] has its own interest in the gathering. Its Project Entrainment means to reach the source of the [[Phenomena/Harmony/Voidsong|Voidsong]] and change what emits it, and the message the Party read about the pilgrimage was part of that project. The company's logs speak of sending its own people in among the guests.

With the strider finished, the guests who hold [[Phenomena/Keystone|keystones]] are carried up in shipping crates beside one of its legs, each given a plot of land a quarter mile on a side, and each plants the stone in a ritual circle there to grow from it a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]]. The Providence is to depart six days later, with a banquet the evening it leaves, and the host will meet the guests then; he also receives them one at a time by request before that. Its first guests, at berth 6, are the [[Org/Hildebrandt Corporation/index|Hildebrandt Corporation]].

The crossing is not free. The Master of Ceremonies asks a tithe of energy from every guest aboard, a little every week or so, to keep the strider moving in the right direction. It is drawn from anyone aboard, whether or not they hear the Voidsong; the ones who hear it are used to steer. Energy settled in a magic item, or life force, can pay it where the thin magic of a spell cannot. Pulling the energy out damages [[Phenomena/Weave|the weave]] and leaves the whole of the strider fragile, so a fracture can open where a guest cannot pay. The keystone each guest planted glows and shows a number that falls as the tithe is taken. A guest who cannot pay is wrung out on the spot, his body twisting until it tears and runs with a black fluid, and creatures come out of the pool. On the evening the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]] left Ragged Roost its rings spun up for the first step of the crossing and the first tithe was asked, and [[Org/Garrick Textiles/People/Letov Obratz|Letov Obratz]], who had taken his sick nephew's place aboard, died of it.

By the crossing's early days the tithe had risen from 20 to 21, and the guests came to understand it as a fixed sum shared among all who held hearts aboard, so that every guest who fell left the rest to pay more. It was that arithmetic that drove the smaller Orgs to merge their hearts under one.
